

[38;5;231m⏺[39m Good — consent-gated changes the shape of this meaningfully: if no id exists before consent, then [38;5;153mlogin[39m has to work
  correctly [3mwithout[0m a [38;5;153muserId[39m, and "absent" becomes a first-class state rather than an error. I'll carry that through the
  design.

  Last round of questions, then I'll put approaches in front of you.

  [1mThe fork:[0m how much of the tracking pipeline is in this build. This one is cheap to change later — adding event
  recording on top of a clean identity module is additive — which is exactly why I'd rather not bundle it.

  - [1mIdentity only[0m — a module that mints, stores, reads, and clears the id behind a consent gate; [38;5;153mlogin[39m takes [38;5;153muserId[39m and
    includes it in the logging it already does. Unblocks the other forms you mentioned, and is small enough to get
    right. Doesn't yet answer "where do login events go" — that stays [38;5;153mconsole.log[39m.
  - [1mIdentity + event recording[0m — also a place login events accumulate (a buffer, or a POST when the API is real).
    Answers the original "track who logged in" end to end, but drags in event schema, retention, and failure handling,
    and the transport can't be settled while the backend is a stub.

  [1mMy recommendation: identity only for this spec.[0m The identity is the part every other form depends on and the part
  that's costly to get wrong; event recording is additive and will be better designed once there's a real endpoint to
  send to. I'd rather ship the foundation than guess at a schema.

